          %h3#cup_product_in_deligne_cohomology Cup product in Deligne cohomology
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            refines to Deligne cohomology.
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beilinson-Deligne+cup-product"} Beilinson-Deligne cup-product
          %h2#Properties Properties
          %h3#CharacteristicMaps Curvature and characteristic classes
          %p
            We discuss the construction of two canonical morphisms out of Deligne cohomology, and two canonical morphisms into it. Below these are shown to form two interlocking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
            and in fact an exact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"} differential cohomology hexagon
            which accurately characterizes Deligne cohomology as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            extension of integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
          %p
            Throughout, for ease of notation, we assume
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            to be positive,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  n \geq 1
                  \\,.
          %p
            The remaining case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            describes “circle 0-bundles with connection”, which are just
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
          %p
            In the following
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
          #UnderlyingBundleMap.num_defn
