                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_+(Smooth0Type)
              considered here indeed may be understood as taking place in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HomotopyPullbacksPreservedbyInclusionIntoAllSmoothHomotopyTypes"}
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"} differential cohomology hexagon
          %h3#GAGA GAGA
          %p
            The Deligne complex is naturally defined in smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            as well as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+geometry"} complex analytic geometry
            as well as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            over the complex numbers. In the spirit of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GAGA"} GAGA
            it is of interest to know how Deligne cohomology in these different settings relates.
          %p
            One useful statement is: given an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} algebraic variety
            over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytification"} analytification
            to lift to an algebraic Deligne cocycle is that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+form"} curvature form
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+form"} algebraic form
            (
            %a{:href => "#Esnault89"}> Esnault 89, corollary 1.3
            ).
          %h3#moduli_and_deformation_theory Moduli and deformation theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            of holomorphic Deligne cohomology groups are closely related to
            = succeed "," do
